# Project: Web Virtual Eyewear Try-On

Stack: Next.js 15 (App Router), TypeScript strict mode, React Three Fiber + drei,
@mediapipe/tasks-vision, Tailwind CSS.

## Non-negotiables
- Never fabricate a face-tracking algorithm — use @mediapipe/tasks-vision's FaceLandmarker
  task and its documented output shape (landmarks, blendshapes, facialTransformationMatrixes).
  Verify the current API by checking node_modules/@mediapipe/tasks-vision types before writing
  code against it — do not assume method names from training data.
- All 3D rendering goes through React Three Fiber, not raw imperative Three.js, unless there's
  a specific documented reason (state it in a comment).
- Color management: renderer must use sRGB output color space and ACESFilmic (or Neutral) tone
  mapping. Do not skip this — flag it explicitly in any component that creates a WebGLRenderer.
- Every glasses model gets positioned/scaled/rotated via a single `useFitEngine` hook — no
  scattered position math in components.
- Before importing any npm package, verify it's installed and check its actual exported API
  in node_modules — do not assume APIs from memory.

## Working style
- Build in the phased order described in the project brief (tracking MVP → basic fit → real
  asset+materials → occlusion+smoothing → catalog UI → mobile perf → QA). Do not jump ahead to
  polish before the current phase's core mechanic is visibly working.
- After each phase, stop and summarize what changed and how to test it manually, don't chain
  multiple phases into one uninterrupted run.
- Flag any performance-risky code (anything running per-frame) explicitly in comments.
